# Sprint board — Executing
#
# The agile half of the hybrid framework: predictive phases, with sprints
# inside Executing. Cards are work packages from the WBS, pulled into a sprint
# and moved across the board as they progress.

screen: board
title: Sprint Board
phase: Executing
process: 4.3 Direct and Manage Project Work
order: 70
requires: scope
teaches: |
  How the agile half of a hybrid project works: pulling work rather than
  pushing it, limiting work in progress, and why finishing beats starting. By
  the end you should be able to explain what a WIP limit is for.
before: |
  Work packages, so the cards are the project's actual work rather than
  whatever came up this week. And the charter, because a board full of work
  nobody authorized is the fastest way to burn a month.
after: |
  Status reporting. A board moved honestly is the raw material of every
  status report you will write.
intro: |
  The board is where the plan becomes work in progress. Each card is one piece
  of work; each column is a state it can be in; moving a card right is the only
  honest way to report progress.

  Two rules make a board work rather than decorate a wall. Pull, don't push:
  work is taken when there is capacity, not assigned into a queue. And limit
  work in progress: a column with a WIP limit forces finishing over starting,
  which is what actually shortens delivery time.
source: |
  Boards are not a PMBOK artifact. They come from lean and Kanban practice, and
  the PMBOK Guide 6th ed. covers them under adaptive and agile approaches; the
  work itself is 4.3 Direct and Manage Project Work, and what the board reports
  is 4.5 Monitor and Control Project Work. The Agile Practice Guide, published
  with the 6th edition, is the companion reference.

field sprint_goal
  prompt: What is this iteration committed to achieving?
  check words>=6: A sprint with no goal can only be counted, not judged. The goal is also what you protect when something has to be cut.
  label: Current sprint goal
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    Agile Practice Guide: the sprint goal, agreed at sprint planning, is the
    one outcome the iteration is committed to.
  why: |
    A sprint with a goal can be judged. A sprint that is only a list of cards
    can only be counted. The goal is also what you protect when something has
    to be cut mid-sprint.
  if_blank: |
    The iteration becomes a batch of unrelated tasks, and "did it go well"
    turns into "how many did we finish".
  example: |
    Sprint 2: every Planning screen usable end to end on this project's own
    record.

field cards
  prompt: What is the actual work, card by card?
  check rows>=3: A board with fewer than three cards is a to-do list.
  check column:state: Every card has to be in a column. A card with no state is work nobody can see.
  label: Board
  type: table
  origin: yours
  view: board
  group_by: state
  title_column: title
  subtitle_column: owner
  tone_column: priority
  source: |
    Not a PMBOK artifact. A Kanban board, used here as the Executing-phase view
    of the work packages defined in 5.4 Create WBS.
  why: |
    Each card should be small enough to finish inside one iteration and be
    worth something on its own. The estimate column is deliberately in points,
    not hours: points compare cards to each other, which people are good at,
    instead of predicting the future, which people are bad at.

    Blocked is its own flag rather than a column, because a blocked card still
    occupies the state it is stuck in, and hiding that would make the WIP count
    lie.
  if_blank: |
    Status is whatever was said in the last conversation, and the same piece of
    work gets started twice.
  example: |
    C4 | Help panel shows source and why | Doing | Sprint 2 | Delivery team | 3 | High
  column ref
    label: ID
    type: line
    width: 1
  column title
    label: Card
    type: line
    width: 5
  column state
    label: Column
    type: choice
    options: Backlog=faint, Ready=neutral, Doing=accent, Review=warn, Done=ok
    wip: Doing=3, Review=2
    width: 2
  column sprint
    label: Sprint
    type: line
    width: 2
  column owner
    label: Owner
    type: line
    width: 2
  column points
    label: Points
    type: number
    width: 1
  column priority
    label: Priority
    type: choice
    options: High=danger, Medium=warn, Low=faint
    width: 1
  column blocked
    label: Blocked
    type: choice
    options: No=faint, Yes=danger
    width: 1
  column detail
    label: Detail
    type: text
    width: 5
  column acceptance
    label: Done when
    type: text
    width: 4

field definition_of_done
  prompt: What has to be true of every card before it counts as finished?
  check filled: Without one, 'done' gets renegotiated per card and the gap surfaces later as rework.
  label: Definition of done
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    Agile Practice Guide: the definition of done, the team's shared checklist
    that every card must satisfy before it can be called finished.
  why: |
    It is written once and applies to every card, which stops "done" from being
    renegotiated per card. It is also the cheapest quality control a team has.
  if_blank: |
    Cards reach the Done column in different states of finished, and the gap
    surfaces later as rework.
  example: |
    Builds clean, runs from a fresh install, the field's help panel is written,
    and the record file still diffs cleanly.
